        content: 'Once you have labor and material costs totaled, apply your overhead allocation and profit margin. Overhead allocation is typically expressed as a percentage of direct costs or revenue and covers all the expenses that don\'t tie directly to a specific job: office rent, software, marketing, administrative time, and equipment ownership costs beyond what\'s captured in labor rates. A common approach is to add a fifteen to twenty percent overhead factor on top of direct costs, then add your target profit margin on top of that. The total gives you your job price. Double-check the margin by dividing profit by revenue — not by cost. A job with one thousand dollars in costs and a two hundred dollar profit margin is a sixteen-point-seven percent margin on revenue, not twenty percent on cost. This distinction matters when comparing margins across jobs and tracking performance against targets. Your proposal format should be professional, clear, and complete. Use a written proposal for any job over five hundred dollars. The proposal should include the full scope of work in plain language, the price, payment terms, scheduled dates, any exclusions the client might assume are included, and a signature line for client approval. Vague scope descriptions are a common source of client disputes. "Install mulch in front beds" is vague. "Install three inches of hardwood mulch in all front-of-house planting beds, approximately four hundred and fifty square feet, edging beds before application" is specific and protects both parties. Follow up on open proposals within forty-eight hours. Many jobs are lost not because the price was wrong but because a competitor responded faster. Build proposal follow-up into your weekly routine.'
