        content: 'Price increases are the moment when retention strategy gets tested. Handle them well and most clients stay. Handle them poorly and you create a reason for clients who were already lukewarm to finally make the switch they\'d been considering. The key is timing, framing, and confidence. Announce price increases in writing with at least thirty days notice. A letter or email that explains the reason — rising fuel costs, equipment costs, or wage adjustments — and expresses appreciation for the client\'s business is far more effective than a surprise invoice at a new rate. Clients understand that costs rise. What they don\'t tolerate is feeling blindsided. Frame increases as consistent with your commitment to quality. "To continue providing the level of service you expect, we\'re adjusting our pricing effective March 1. Your monthly maintenance will move from two hundred forty to two hundred fifty-five dollars." The increase is positioned as enabling quality, not as a hit to their wallet. Don\'t apologize for the increase. Apologizing signals uncertainty about whether the new price is justified, which invites negotiation. Confidence in your pricing conveys that the price is fair and that you\'re secure enough in your service quality not to need to justify it defensively. For long-tenured clients who have been with you five or more years, consider a loyalty rate that holds their increase below the standard level. A client who\'s been paying on time for six years and who refers neighbors to you is worth a gesture of appreciation. Note it in your communication: "As a thank-you for your continued loyalty, we\'re holding your increase below our standard adjustment." This small acknowledgment creates significant goodwill.'
