        content: 'The first thirty to ninety days of a landscape relationship are the highest-churn period. Clients who are not satisfied with how the relationship started are far more likely to cancel in the first season than clients who had a strong initial experience. Proactive touchpoints during this period serve as both quality checks and relationship investments that reduce early cancellation significantly.\n\nSchedule a thirty-day check-in call or message with every new client. This does not need to be a long conversation: a brief message asking how the first few visits have been, whether anything needs to be adjusted, and confirming that the service schedule is working for them communicates that you are attentive and care about their satisfaction. Most clients will respond positively, and the small percentage with a concern gives you the opportunity to address it before it becomes a reason to cancel.\n\nSend a welcome gift or a value-added service during the first season. A complimentary service add-on, a free lawn assessment report with plant health observations, or a small branded gift makes the new client feel appreciated and differentiates your company from competitors who simply send an invoice. The cost of a welcome gesture is small relative to the lifetime value of a client who renews for three or five years, and it creates a memorable positive experience early in the relationship.\n\nAsk for feedback at the end of the first season, before the renewal conversation. Understanding what worked, what did not, and what the client wished had been different gives you information to improve the service and demonstrates that you view the relationship as a continuous improvement process rather than a fixed transaction. Clients who feel that their feedback is welcome and acted upon are significantly more loyal than those who feel their only option is to cancel if they are not satisfied. Use the feedback to inform the renewal conversation and to set up the next season for an even stronger result.'
