        content: 'Communication between the office and the field breaks down when it relies too heavily on phone calls. Calls interrupt crews mid-task, get missed when someone\'s running equipment, and leave no documentation trail. A better model routes most field communication through a centralized messaging layer in your scheduling or dispatching app. Messages about job changes, special instructions, or scheduling updates get delivered as notifications that crews can read between stops rather than mid-task.\n\nFor urgent communication that genuinely requires a response, the app should support two-way messaging so the dispatcher and crew can exchange information quickly without the overhead of a phone call. Some operations also use group messaging channels by crew so the dispatcher can reach an entire team with a single message. Whatever tools you use, the key is establishing communication norms: what goes in the app, what warrants a call, and how quickly crews are expected to acknowledge job updates. Without those norms, you end up with some crews responding instantly and others going dark until end of day. Consistency in field communication is a habit that needs to be built intentionally, and it starts with giving crews tools that make communication easier than ignoring it.',
