        content: 'General liability insurance is the foundational coverage every lawn and landscape business needs. It protects you when your work causes property damage or bodily injury to a third party — a mower throws a rock through a window, a crew member\'s equipment damages an underground pipe, or a client trips over equipment left in a pathway. Most residential and commercial clients require proof of general liability coverage before signing a contract. Minimum coverage levels vary by client type — residential clients may accept one million dollars per occurrence, while commercial property managers often require two million or higher. Buy coverage at the level your clients require, not at the minimum you can get away with. Property coverage for your equipment and vehicles is separate from general liability. Commercial property coverage protects your mowers, trailers, tools, and any equipment stored at your facility. Make sure the coverage value reflects the current replacement cost of your fleet — equipment that was purchased four years ago at lower prices may be significantly more expensive to replace today. Review your coverage limits annually and update as you add equipment or expand your operations. An insurance policy that hasn\'t been reviewed in three years may not reflect the assets you\'re actually operating with.'
