        content: 'Commercial landscape maintenance clients — property management companies, real estate investment trusts, retail center operators, and corporate campus managers — evaluate landscape service primarily on appearance consistency and professionalism rather than horticultural results. A missed visit, a section of unedged sidewalk, or a cluster of visible weeds near a building entrance can generate a formal complaint or a financial penalty under some commercial contracts. Understanding this dynamic before you take on commercial work is essential. Commercial contracts typically specify appearance standards in greater detail than residential contracts. They may require that all hard surface edges be maintained to a defined specification, that trash and debris be removed from planting beds at every visit regardless of accumulation level, that irrigation system issues be reported within twenty-four hours, and that the crew communicate with the property manager before accessing any area with active pedestrian or vehicle traffic. Compliance with these specifications is what earns you contract renewal and referrals to additional properties in the management portfolio. Commercial properties also have higher liability exposure. A slip-and-fall claim on a property you maintain can name your company as a defendant regardless of whether the specific cause was related to your work. Carry commercial general liability insurance at the limit specified in the contract — typically one to two million dollars per occurrence with an umbrella policy — and name the property management company and property owner as additionally insured parties as required. Certificate of insurance requirements are non-negotiable for most commercial accounts and need to be kept current and delivered promptly when requested. Visit frequency for commercial properties during the growing season is typically weekly, and some high-profile accounts specify five-day-per-week service or even daily maintenance during special events or the peak business season. Price these accounts based on actual visit frequency and time rather than applying a residential pricing model to a larger property.'
