        content: 'Homeowners associations are governed by elected boards and managed either by the board members themselves or by a professional property management company. Understanding who makes decisions and who has day-to-day authority over the landscape maintenance contract is essential before you begin work and during the account relationship. In a self-managed HOA, the board president or a designated landscape committee chair is typically your primary contact. In a professionally managed HOA, your daily contact is the community manager employed by the management company, and the board reviews performance at monthly or quarterly meetings. Always establish clear relationships with both levels — the community manager for operational questions and the board or landscape chair for contract approvals and renewal decisions. HOA landscape maintenance contracts are often put out for competitive bid on a one to three year cycle. The bid process is more formal than a residential quote — you may be required to submit a written proposal responding to a scope of work document, provide references from similar HOA accounts, carry specified insurance limits, and present in person to the board or committee. Invest in professional bid materials for HOA contracts. A well-formatted proposal that clearly addresses every specification in their scope document, includes site photos demonstrating your understanding of the property, and presents pricing in a clear, organized format wins more bids than a generic quote sheet. When structuring the contract, include a detailed scope of work that addresses every common area in the HOA — entrance features, amenity areas, retention ponds, common area turf, perimeter plantings, and any individual homeowner areas that are covered by the association\'s contract. The more specific your scope, the fewer disputes you will have about what is and is not included. Include a change order process for work outside the scope so that additional services are captured as revenue rather than absorbed as courtesy work that erodes your margin.'
